WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.201 --> 00:00:02.760
(lighthearted music)

00:00:02.760 --> 00:00:05.170
- You know they don't tell
you, "Don't lay on the bed."

00:00:05.170 --> 00:00:06.870
You're supposed to lay on the bed.

00:00:07.790 --> 00:00:10.170
- [Narrator] If you've
ever visited an Ikea,

00:00:10.170 --> 00:00:11.180
you've likely encountered

00:00:11.180 --> 00:00:14.300
their store's endlessly
winding floor plan.

00:00:14.300 --> 00:00:19.300
This layout can be confusing,
but that is by design.

00:00:19.570 --> 00:00:22.980
Ikea may be the biggest
furniture retailer on the planet

00:00:22.980 --> 00:00:25.410
but it's certainly not conventional.

00:00:25.410 --> 00:00:28.040
The Swedish furniture
giant asks its customers

00:00:28.040 --> 00:00:29.730
to build their own products

00:00:29.730 --> 00:00:32.440
and it stores and
distributes those products

00:00:32.440 --> 00:00:35.090
in minimalist flat packed boxes.

00:00:35.090 --> 00:00:37.700
So, how have these retail strategies,

00:00:37.700 --> 00:00:39.910
as well as their famous Swedish meatballs

00:00:39.910 --> 00:00:43.170
contributed to the
company's runaway success?

00:00:43.170 --> 00:00:46.360
This is the economics of Ikea,

00:00:46.360 --> 00:00:48.690
a look at the innovative
business practices

00:00:48.690 --> 00:00:51.053
that have transformed modern life.

00:00:52.260 --> 00:00:53.710
- [Sarah] When you step into an Ikea,

00:00:53.710 --> 00:00:58.520
you immediately sort of
are put into this maze like

00:00:58.520 --> 00:01:01.120
path of different rooms.

00:01:01.120 --> 00:01:04.350
- [Narrator] Ikea's store
layout is a fixed path design

00:01:04.350 --> 00:01:06.280
which means there's a designated road

00:01:06.280 --> 00:01:08.170
that all customers must follow

00:01:08.170 --> 00:01:11.430
that guides you through
the store in one direction.

00:01:11.430 --> 00:01:13.320
- [Sarah] It's not a grab a carton of milk

00:01:13.320 --> 00:01:16.773
and get out kind of store,
it's the opposite of that.

00:01:18.320 --> 00:01:21.510
It's very much set up to spend a day,

00:01:21.510 --> 00:01:25.030
think about rooms you know,
dream about what you really want

00:01:25.030 --> 00:01:26.543
your bedroom to look like.

00:01:28.000 --> 00:01:30.600
- [Narrator] The floor
plan of most Ikea stores

00:01:30.600 --> 00:01:33.760
resembles a maze that
curves about every 50 feet

00:01:33.760 --> 00:01:37.060
to keep customers curious
about what comes next.

00:01:37.060 --> 00:01:40.900
Since an average Ikea store
is around 300,000 square feet

00:01:40.900 --> 00:01:43.010
or five American football fields,

00:01:43.010 --> 00:01:44.960
that means a lot of walking.

00:01:44.960 --> 00:01:48.744
- An Ikea is to some frustratingly winding

00:01:48.744 --> 00:01:51.430
but really it's laid out as an experience

00:01:51.430 --> 00:01:52.792
to get you to buy more.

00:01:52.792 --> 00:01:54.600
(drill buzzing)

00:01:54.600 --> 00:01:55.870
- [Narrator] Ikea is famous

00:01:55.870 --> 00:01:57.960
for putting its customers to work.

00:01:57.960 --> 00:01:59.560
Unlike most furniture retailers

00:01:59.560 --> 00:02:03.260
that sell products preassembled,
many of Ikea's pieces

00:02:03.260 --> 00:02:05.830
have to be built by their customers.

00:02:05.830 --> 00:02:06.870
But why?

00:02:06.870 --> 00:02:09.310
As many couples and their
therapists will know

00:02:09.310 --> 00:02:12.570
building your own Ikea
cabinet can be challenging.

00:02:12.570 --> 00:02:15.960
- The big idea behind the Ikea effect is

00:02:15.960 --> 00:02:19.370
consumers are more attached
to have more positive feelings

00:02:19.370 --> 00:02:22.930
towards objects or things
that we've put effort into.

00:02:22.930 --> 00:02:24.650
And that we actually think

00:02:24.650 --> 00:02:26.800
that they're more
valuable because of that.

00:02:28.200 --> 00:02:32.160
- [Narrator] The term Ikea
effect was first coined in 2011

00:02:32.160 --> 00:02:34.920
by researchers who noticed
a similar phenomena

00:02:34.920 --> 00:02:37.540
in other products and businesses.

00:02:37.540 --> 00:02:41.300
When instant cake mixes were
first introduced in the 1950s

00:02:41.300 --> 00:02:42.550
they didn't sell well.

00:02:42.550 --> 00:02:45.310
- And then they said,
"Let's add a fresh egg."

00:02:45.310 --> 00:02:47.470
It was this idea that we wanna feel

00:02:47.470 --> 00:02:50.350
like we're just participating
enough to not feel guilty

00:02:50.350 --> 00:02:51.793
about taking a shortcut.

00:02:52.880 --> 00:02:55.830
- [Man] When you make a cake
from a mix which do you want?

00:02:55.830 --> 00:02:59.040
A fresh egg cake or a
cake made with dried eggs?

00:02:59.040 --> 00:03:03.030
A higher, lighter, tastier
cake, why fresh eggs of course.

00:03:03.030 --> 00:03:06.080
- The idea that we should
love building products

00:03:06.080 --> 00:03:08.763
isn't necessarily what Ikea intended.

00:03:09.710 --> 00:03:11.390
- [Narrator] If you've ever
shopped at one of Ikea's

00:03:11.390 --> 00:03:13.230
massive warehouse stores

00:03:13.230 --> 00:03:17.020
you're likely aware of the
unconventional product names

00:03:17.020 --> 00:03:20.140
but what you may not realize
is that in creating these items

00:03:20.140 --> 00:03:22.420
Ikea sometimes comes up
with the price tag first.

00:03:22.420 --> 00:03:24.670
- So we have a classic
example that they could talk

00:03:24.670 --> 00:03:27.650
about all the time is the $1 light bulb.

00:03:27.650 --> 00:03:30.780
But they had this idea
that a $1 LED light bulb,

00:03:30.780 --> 00:03:32.420
you know this new type of light bulb

00:03:32.420 --> 00:03:34.410
would be hard to achieve,
but if they could achieve it,

00:03:34.410 --> 00:03:36.720
lots of people would buy LED light bulbs.

00:03:36.720 --> 00:03:38.880
So they just sort of designed backward

00:03:39.760 --> 00:03:41.610
with the price point in mind.

00:03:41.610 --> 00:03:43.280
- [Narrator] That
obsession with low prices

00:03:43.280 --> 00:03:46.150
is a large part of why Ikea is the world's

00:03:46.150 --> 00:03:48.290
largest furniture retailer.

00:03:48.290 --> 00:03:53.290
Today, Ikea has 445 stores
operating in 52 countries.

00:03:53.660 --> 00:03:55.880
- You know obviously if you
go into a student dorm room,

00:03:55.880 --> 00:03:57.580
you're gonna find a lot of Ikea,

00:03:57.580 --> 00:03:59.720
but you'll also find some Ikea products

00:03:59.720 --> 00:04:01.890
in a wealthy person's home.

00:04:01.890 --> 00:04:03.940
And that's really what they're going for.

00:04:06.440 --> 00:04:08.790
- [Narrator] Today, Ikea
is the very definition

00:04:08.790 --> 00:04:12.210
of mass market appeal, but
when the company first began

00:04:12.210 --> 00:04:15.200
as a Swedish mail order business in 1943,

00:04:15.200 --> 00:04:17.930
well-designed furniture
tended to be expensive.

00:04:17.930 --> 00:04:20.480
And as a result out of reach for most,

00:04:20.480 --> 00:04:23.173
or seen as a serious long-term investment.

00:04:24.140 --> 00:04:27.610
Ingvar Kamprad, who founded
the company as a teenager,

00:04:27.610 --> 00:04:30.900
pushed forward the idea that
furniture could be flat packed

00:04:30.900 --> 00:04:34.360
to massively reduce the cost
of shipping and transportation.

00:04:34.360 --> 00:04:38.070
- So flat packing is
really the largest arguably

00:04:38.070 --> 00:04:42.820
Ikea invention that really
led to the company's growth.

00:04:42.820 --> 00:04:45.610
And the idea is that
instead of buying, you know,

00:04:45.610 --> 00:04:48.020
a piece of furniture I'll put
together, it's deconstructed

00:04:48.020 --> 00:04:50.270
into a flat pack, where you
can fit more in a truck.

00:04:50.270 --> 00:04:52.160
You can fit more in the Ikea warehouse

00:04:52.160 --> 00:04:54.210
and you can also get it in your car.

00:04:54.210 --> 00:04:55.680
And the trade-off is you know

00:04:55.680 --> 00:04:57.230
you put it together at the end.

00:04:57.230 --> 00:04:59.850
- [Narrator] Flat packing
is a practical aspect

00:04:59.850 --> 00:05:02.990
of the philosophy that has
long guided Ikea's success

00:05:02.990 --> 00:05:05.050
called democratic design.

00:05:05.050 --> 00:05:06.620
- It's this idea that
everything is imbalanced

00:05:06.620 --> 00:05:09.260
both price, form, function, the aesthetic,

00:05:09.260 --> 00:05:10.400
the sustainability.

00:05:10.400 --> 00:05:11.300
- [Narrator] This vision

00:05:11.300 --> 00:05:14.400
to create a better everyday
life for the many people

00:05:14.400 --> 00:05:17.590
was sent forth more than
30 years ago by Kamprad

00:05:17.590 --> 00:05:21.230
in a manifesto now presented
to every Ikea employee.

00:05:21.230 --> 00:05:23.710
- And they talk about
it almost religiously,

00:05:23.710 --> 00:05:26.250
and fundamentally it's this idea

00:05:26.250 --> 00:05:29.200
that when designing a
product they think about

00:05:29.200 --> 00:05:31.520
it can't just be really cool looking,

00:05:31.520 --> 00:05:32.630
it can't just be functional,

00:05:32.630 --> 00:05:34.280
it has to be all of those things.

00:05:35.150 --> 00:05:37.610
- [Narrator] So despite
the long shopping trips

00:05:37.610 --> 00:05:42.610
and the DIY, customers can't
seem to get enough of Ikea.

00:05:42.640 --> 00:05:45.723
Perhaps it's as simple
as labor leaves to love.

00:05:45.723 --> 00:05:48.890
(lighthearted music)

